old string risky. We can write a new small Python patch script that reads file and replaces text between `# cat shadow on cushion` and `add('</g>')` before `# ------------------------------------------------------------------ TABLE`. But the instructions prefer edit. Yet targeted edits with big block are okay. We can use `edit` with old_string from line 663 to 703. That's long. Alternatively use a Python script to perform replacement, but instructions say use edit. We should follow. Use edit with old string maybe truncated? Must be exact. We'll copy.\n\nBefore that, maybe improve table grain. Easy.\n\nCurtains: remove embroidery sprigs? We can alter the for-loop blocks to create denser small flower motifs. Maybe simpler remove the twig groups and add subtle vertical woven stripes. There are two blocks. Could replace with a few small floral clusters. Let's make pattern with tiny dots and leaves, denser. Or use a stencil-like border at bottom. Current sparse twigs read as random marks. I think remove them entirely and add woven vertical texture + a small embroidered border near the bottom (a vine with leaves). That looks intentional.\n\n- Left curtain: remove 5 sprig groups; add bottom vine border along y=590? Curtain bottom 632, border at y=600: wavy stem across x=480..545 with leaves. But the curtain is only 100px wide; a vertical vine border along inner edge could look nice. Let's add vertical vine at x=560, from 160 to 600, with leaves every 30. And maybe small floral dots. That would be coherent.\n- Or keep curtain plain with folds and add a tieback. Plain curtains are elegant. Remove sprigs, add subtle slub texture lines. Do that. It will be less noisy.\n\nWindow view: need enhance. Let's edit view section. Add:\n- more atmospheric distant hills layers with haze.\n- A field with rows, small flowers.\n- A garden path curving from bottom center to horizon.\n- More trees and shrubs, varied sizes.\n- A watering can? maybe.\n- Sun at upper right with rays (subtle).\n- Birds (3).\n- Maybe a cat in the garden? no.\n\nCurrent view is large; adding path and flowers gives depth. Let's add a winding path from bottom center (y=586) to horizon (x=820,y=470), pale cream. Add two flower beds on sides. Add fence. Good.\n- Also add a stone wall? maybe.\n- Add soft haze at horizon (cream band).\n- Add tree leaves highlights.\n\nLet's modify view section by inserting after meadow and before fence. Need careful layering: path over meadow, but below fence? The fence at y=563 across. The path should go through fence maybe. We could put path before fence and have fence overlay. Fine. Flowers around.\n\nWindow frame: add thickness and inner shadow. Already.\n\nBacksplash tiles look nice.\n\nBreakfast: improve food. Let's replace toast group maybe. Current toast group. Could add:\n- crust shading, seeds, a small tomato slice, basil leaf.\n- Second slice with butter melting.\n- Add a small ramekin of berries? Perhaps at (1010, 930) behind plate? But vase. Maybe add a tiny bowl of strawberries at (960, 900) near plate right; however butter and oranges there. The table already busy. Keep.\n- Add a glass of orange juice at (1000, 920) (behind plate to right). It adds morning. But would overlap jam jar? no. Glass at x=1000 y=900, vase at 1120. Plate right edge 1007, so place glass at 1040, 900 behind plate. But oranges at 1046,1019. Hmm. Could put juice glass at (1045, 900), behind plate and left of vase. It might overlap plate rim but okay. Draw after plate? Maybe in front. Let's add small amber juice glass with orange slice and straw at (1040, 913). That may crowd. Alternatively remove butter crock and put glass there. Butter crock is low impact. Replace butter with a small pot of strawberry jam? Hmm.\n\nCould add a small bowl of berries at (960, 1040) instead of butter. Berries + yogurt. But current butter crock adds cream. Maybe add a couple strawberries around plate. \n- Add a tiny vase? already.\n- Add honey dipper? no.\n\nThe biggest issue is the plate looks flat and the toast is simplistic. Improve by adding a third toast and a little side salad. Add shadow under food on plate. Add a knife. Add a small dish of jam or butter on plate. Add flaky salt. Use gradients. \n- Current plate has two toast slices and egg. Add:\n  - a few arugula leaves at right.\n  - cherry tomato halves at left.\n  - a small ramekin of berries at top? \n- We can make food more dimensional with highlights and shadows.\n\nAlso the plate rim blue line is a nice touch.\n\nMug: could be larger and more detailed. Fine.\n\nSteam: make more visible and integrate with light. Increase opacity to .7 and add glow. Already .55 but blurred; in render subtle. Add one more steam curl.\n\nLighting: create stronger rays. We'll update beam section with more visible shafts and a warm overall gradient. Let's test.\n\nPostprocess can add color contrast and maybe a bit of grain. Currently raw is better because postprocessing washes out? Postprocess made more contrast but also glow. Could refine later.\n\nLet's make edits incrementally, render, inspect.\n\nStep 1: Table grain.\nReplace:\n```\nfor i in range(16): ...\nfor i in range(8): ...\n```\nwith more organic:\n- 10 horizontal curves with varying opacity.\n- 4 long elliptical swirls.\n- 2 knots: ellipse rings at (1000, 940) and (620, 1080)? But knots might look like eyes. Keep subtle.\n- Remove vertical lines.\nLet's do:\n```\nfor i in range(11):\n y=870+i*24\n add(path with C curves, stroke #7f4a2e, width 1.4, opacity .12)\nfor i in range(4):\n add path long arc...\n```\nAdd top sheen.\n\nStep 2: Cat.\n\nStep 3: Curtains remove sparse sprigs and add subtle slub.\n\nStep 4: Window view path & depth.\n\nStep 5: Beams.\n\nStep 6: Breakfast.\n\nMaybe add a rug? no.\n\nLet's edit table grain first."}
